-- ===========================================================================
-- 0002_issuance_attempt_ledger — `issuance_attempts` learns how to count, and
-- `supplier_requests` learns who answered.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit from packages/db/src/schema (the new `attempt`,
-- `probe_count` and `provider` columns and their constraints), then edited by
-- hand in exactly two ways, both deliberate and both explained below:
--
--   1. the three `ADD COLUMN`s were given `DEFAULT` values and an immediately
--      following `DROP DEFAULT`, because drizzle-kit emits a bare
--      `ADD COLUMN ... NOT NULL` that cannot succeed on a table with rows; and
--   2. the statements were REORDERED, because drizzle-kit put the `DROP INDEX`
--      first and it must come last (see ORDERING below).
--
-- Everything else — the annotations — follows 0000_init and 0001: this file,
-- not the TypeScript, is what a reviewer who does not know Drizzle reads.
--
-- WHAT THIS BUYS
--
-- Phase 3's retry policy is a pure function of the rows in `issuance_attempts`.
-- Reasoning over them needs two numbers the table does not yet hold:
--
--   * `attempt`  — which attempt this is FOR THIS ORDER. It exists today only
--                  as a text segment inside `request_id`, and the alternative
--                  to a column is `split_part(request_id, '_', -1)::int`:
--                  string surgery on a value whose other segments contain the
--                  same delimiter, unindexable, and silently wrong the day the
--                  id shape changes.
--   * `probe_count` — how many times ONE request_id has been sent, the first
--                  ask included. A re-probe after a timeout is the same
--                  question to the same supplier under the same id, so it
--                  deliberately does not create a second row; the bound on
--                  re-probes therefore has to be a counter in a column.
--
-- and `supplier_requests` needs to record which supplier answered, so a probe
-- addressed to the wrong one misses instead of being answered with the other's
-- code.
--
-- MEASURED (Postgres 16.11, this project's container, inside a rolled-back
-- transaction against the 21 200-attempt fixture — spec 003
-- technical-considerations §10):
--
--     ADD COLUMN attempt integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 1 ..... 1.7 ms  metadata only
--     ALTER COLUMN attempt DROP DEFAULT ................. 0.5 ms  metadata only
--     ADD COLUMN probe_count ........................... metadata only
--     the two CHECKs ................................... 3.3 ms  full scan, no rewrite
--     ADD CONSTRAINT ... UNIQUE (order_id, attempt) .... 17.2 ms  ACCESS EXCLUSIVE
--     DROP INDEX issuance_attempts_order_id_idx ........ instant
--     supplier_requests.provider ....................... metadata only
--
-- NOTHING HERE REWRITES A TABLE. No `ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE`, no volatile
-- default, no `SET NOT NULL` on an existing column. Verified by comparing
-- `pg_class.relfilenode` for both tables before and after: unchanged.
--
-- WHY EVERY `ADD COLUMN` CARRIES A DEFAULT AND THEN LOSES IT
--
-- The default is there for exactly one reason: a `NOT NULL` column added with a
-- **constant** default is metadata-only. Since PG 11 the value is stored once in
-- `pg_attribute.attmissingval` and materialised lazily as rows are read, so no
-- existing row is touched. Without a default the statement simply cannot run on
-- a table with rows — every one of them would violate `NOT NULL`.
--
-- LEAVING THE DEFAULT IN PLACE WOULD BE A SILENT-REUSE BUG, and this is the one
-- paragraph in the file worth reading twice. Suppose `attempt` kept
-- `DEFAULT 1`. A caller that forgot to compute the next attempt number would
-- insert a row saying attempt 1 — no error, nothing in a log. It would then
-- derive `req_{order}_{provider}_1`, an id that already exists and was settled
-- long ago; the reserving INSERT's `ON CONFLICT (request_id) DO NOTHING` would
-- swallow the collision; and the worker would go on to RE-PROBE a finished
-- attempt instead of making a new one. The order can never be re-issued and
-- nothing anywhere says so. (technical-considerations §11, R7.)
--
-- With the default dropped, that same mistake is
--
--     23502 not_null_violation   null value in column "attempt"
--
-- at the first insert. The same argument applies to `supplier_requests.provider`
-- in the opposite direction: a row written by supplier B that forgot to say so
-- would be recorded as `'a'` — B's own ledger lookups would then miss, and B
-- would claim a SECOND key for a request that was already answered. That is
-- precisely the bug the column is being added to prevent, so the column must not
-- be able to fill itself in.
--
-- `probe_count` is dropped too, for consistency and for the same class of
-- reason: a count of asks should be a number somebody wrote on purpose. (Note
-- for whoever implements the ladder: §1.2 of the tech spec quotes an INSERT that
-- passes `default` for `probe_count`. It must pass a literal `1` instead — see
-- the note at the end of this file.)
--
-- BACKFILL VALUES, AND WHY THEY ARE CORRECT RATHER THAN CONVENIENT
--
--   attempt      = 1    Phases 1 and 2 derive exactly one request id per order,
--                       `req_{order}_a_1` (`FIRST_ISSUANCE_ATTEMPT`). Every
--                       existing row IS attempt 1.
--   probe_count  = 1    Every existing row was asked exactly once; nothing in
--                       Phases 1 and 2 re-probes.
--   provider     = 'a'  Supplier A is the only supplier that has ever existed.
--                       B arrives later in this same slice.
--
-- ATOMICITY. `packages/db/src/migrate.ts` calls Drizzle's migrator, whose pg
-- dialect opens ONE transaction spanning every pending file and every
-- `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations` insert — not one transaction per file. (The
-- comment in `migrate.ts` and the header of `0000_init.sql` still say
-- "each migration file runs inside a transaction"; that is the weaker claim and
-- it is inaccurate. The guarantee is stronger, but do not reason as though file
-- N+1 commits independently of file N.) Every statement below therefore commits
-- together or not at all, and the ten of them hold `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` on
-- `issuance_attempts` for the duration — 23 ms on the measured fixture.
--
-- `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` is not available to this project for the same
-- reason: it cannot run inside a transaction block. At these timings the trade
-- is not worth making, and stating the constraint now is cheaper than
-- discovering it during an incident.
--
-- ORDERING — the one thing in this file that is not free to move.
--
--   * The `DROP INDEX` MUST COME AFTER the `ADD CONSTRAINT ... UNIQUE`.
--     `issuance_attempts_order_id_idx` is the only index serving the foreign
--     key's shape until `(order_id, attempt)` exists; dropping it first would
--     leave a window — inside this transaction, but a window — with no index on
--     `order_id` at all. drizzle-kit emitted the DROP first; it was moved.
--   * Each `DROP DEFAULT` follows its own `ADD COLUMN` immediately, so no
--     committed state ever has the default present.
--   * Nothing here depends on 0001, and nothing in 0001 depends on this. Slice
--     4's `orders_undelivered_idx` does depend on 0001's widened CHECK; it is
--     not in this file.
--
-- PRE-FLIGHT, RUN BEFORE THIS MIGRATION AND NOT AFTER IT FAILS. "Add a unique
-- constraint" is the classic migration that passes in development and fails in
-- production, and it is the only statement below that can fail on real data.
-- Every existing row is backfilled to `attempt = 1`, so two attempt rows for
-- one order become two rows reading `(order_id, 1)`:
--
--     SELECT order_id, count(*) FROM issuance_attempts
--     GROUP BY 1 HAVING count(*) > 1;
--     -- 0 rows => the UNIQUE below will succeed.
--     -- any rows => STOP. Those orders carry more than one attempt row, they
--     --             would all be numbered 1, and the ADD CONSTRAINT raises
--     --             23505. Resolve them first; do not weaken the constraint.
--
-- It cannot fail on a database that has only ever run Phases 1 and 2 —
-- `issuance_attempts_request_id_key` plus one derived id per order means at most
-- one attempt row per order — but it is a query, not an argument, so run it.
--
-- Scope: this file is the ledger's columns and constraints and nothing else.
-- `payment_events_paid_order_idx` and `orders_undelivered_idx`
-- (technical-considerations §5) belong to slice 4, and `supplier_behaviour` to
-- the next task in this slice, so each stays independently runnable.
-- ===========================================================================

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (1) `attempt` — which attempt this is FOR THIS ORDER, across every provider.
--
-- Numbered per order, not per provider. The ladder computes `max(attempt) + 1`
-- over ALL of an order's attempts, so falling through from A/1 to B produces
-- attempt 2, and an operator retry after both have refused produces attempt 3.
-- Numbering per provider instead recomputes `req_x_a_1` — a settled id — which
-- is R7 and which the UNIQUE in (5) turns from silence into an error.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTER TABLE "issuance_attempts" ADD COLUMN "attempt" integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;--> statement-breakpoint
-- The default has now done its only job (backfilling 21 200 existing rows
-- without a rewrite) and becomes a liability the moment it survives this file.
-- See the header: a caller that forgets to compute the attempt number must take
-- a 23502, not quietly write 1 and re-probe a finished attempt.
ALTER TABLE "issuance_attempts" ALTER COLUMN "attempt" DROP DEFAULT;--> statement-breakpoint

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (2) `probe_count` — how many times THIS request_id has been sent, the first
--     ask included.
--
-- A re-probe deliberately does not create a second row: same provider, same
-- attempt, therefore the same `request_id`, therefore the same row. The count
-- is what bounds re-probing (`SUPPLIER_MAX_PROBES_PER_REQUEST`, default 3 — one
-- ask and two re-probes), and it is incremented BEFORE the call and outside any
-- transaction, because it counts asks rather than answers: a process killed
-- mid-request leaves a truthful count with no `catch` having had to run.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTER TABLE "issuance_attempts" ADD COLUMN "probe_count" integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 1;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "issuance_attempts" ALTER COLUMN "probe_count" DROP DEFAULT;--> statement-breakpoint

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (3) and (4) The two CHECKs.
--
-- `deriveIssuanceRequestId` already refuses a zero, a float or a NaN before it
-- will build an id — a `req_ord_x_a_NaN` would be a second key issued for one
-- order, found by a customer rather than by a test. These mirror that guard at
-- the layer that still holds when TypeScript is bypassed: a seed, a psql
-- session, a future service in another language.
--
-- Attempt 0 reads as "no attempt" to anyone looking at a log line. A
-- probe_count of 0 would claim a row exists for a request nobody sent, when the
-- row is written precisely because one is about to be.
--
-- Each ADD scans the table to validate; neither rewrites it. They cannot fail
-- here: every row was just set to 1 by (1) and (2).
--
-- Note what is NOT constrained: `provider` and `status` have no CHECK, on
-- purpose and consistently with 0000_init. Those value sets belong to the retry
-- policy, which should not have to alter a constraint in order to extend
-- itself.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTER TABLE "issuance_attempts" ADD CONSTRAINT "issuance_attempts_attempt_positive" CHECK ("attempt" >= 1);--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "issuance_attempts" ADD CONSTRAINT "issuance_attempts_probe_count_positive" CHECK ("probe_count" >= 1);--> statement-breakpoint

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (5) THE CONSTRAINT THIS MIGRATION EXISTS FOR — one attempt number per order.
--
--   UNIQUE (order_id, attempt)
--
-- TWO COLUMNS, NOT THREE. `(order_id, provider, attempt)` was proposed and is
-- wrong given that `attempt` is numbered per order: it would happily accept
-- `(x, a, 3)` and `(x, b, 3)` — two rows both claiming to be attempt 3 of order
-- x — and the ladder's `max(attempt) + 1` would then hand the same number out
-- twice.
--
-- `issuance_attempts_request_id_key` DOES NOT COVER THIS, and the difference is
-- the reason both constraints exist. That one catches two rows carrying the
-- same request id *string*. This one catches a stored `attempt` that has
-- DRIFTED FROM THE STRING IT APPEARS IN — the row whose `request_id` says
-- `req_x_b_3` while its `attempt` column says 2, or two rows whose ids differ
-- only in the provider segment. Without it that drift is silent; with it:
--
--     23505 unique_violation
--     duplicate key value violates unique constraint
--       "issuance_attempts_order_id_attempt_key"
--
-- raised by the INSERT that reserves the attempt — before any supplier is
-- called, and therefore before any key can leave the pool.
--
-- It is also the ladder's read path, which is why it replaces an index rather
-- than adding one:
--
--   SELECT id, request_id, order_id, provider, attempt, status,
--          probe_count, code, last_error, created_at
--   FROM issuance_attempts WHERE order_id = $1 ORDER BY attempt DESC;
--   -- Index Scan Backward using issuance_attempts_order_id_attempt_key,
--   -- 0.012 ms, no Sort node.
--   -- 0 rows => this order has never been offered to a supplier. NOT a
--   --           failure — the recovery list renders it as "not yet attempted",
--   --           which is a different sentence from "the supplier said no".
--   -- This SELECT is read INSIDE the order row lock (I4). Outside it, these
--   -- rows are a snapshot another worker is free to extend between the read
--   -- and the rung computed from it, and two workers computing different
--   -- rungs is how two keys leave the pool for one order.
--
-- `ORDER BY attempt`, not `ORDER BY created_at`: `created_at` defaults to
-- `now()`, which is transaction-start time and therefore TIES for two rows
-- written in one transaction. This constraint is what makes `attempt` a total
-- order with no ties.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTER TABLE "issuance_attempts" ADD CONSTRAINT "issuance_attempts_order_id_attempt_key" UNIQUE("order_id","attempt");--> statement-breakpoint

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (6) The old single-column index, now redundant — AND ONLY NOW.
--
-- `(order_id, attempt)` has `order_id` leading, so every lookup shaped like the
-- foreign key still has an index to use; verified as an Index Only Scan at
-- 0.055 ms. Removes 2.2 MB and one index to maintain on the issuance write
-- path.
--
-- THIS STATEMENT MUST FOLLOW (5). Run first — as drizzle-kit emitted it — it
-- would leave a window with no index on `order_id` at all. Inside one
-- transaction that window is not visible to other sessions, but the ordering is
-- the rule and reversing it in a file that someone later splits would be a real
-- outage.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DROP INDEX "issuance_attempts_order_id_idx";--> statement-breakpoint

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (7) `supplier_requests.provider` — defence in depth on the SUPPLIER's side of
--     the boundary.
--
-- A and B share one fifty-key pool and one ledger table (technical
-- considerations §3, A4: the fifty keys are the assignment's supplied fixture,
-- and the conservation check is one count against another). Sharing the table
-- means the primary key alone is no longer a complete question. The ledger read
-- becomes:
--
--   SELECT code FROM supplier_requests
--   WHERE request_id = $1 AND provider = $2;
--   -- 1 row  => THIS supplier has answered this id before. Return that same
--   --           code, however many times it is asked. This is I5, and it is
--   --           what makes the shop's timeout re-probe safe.
--   -- 0 rows => this supplier has never answered this id: first sight, so
--   --           claim a key. A probe MIS-ADDRESSED to the other supplier lands
--   --           here — 0 rows — instead of being answered with A's code to a
--   --           question that was asked of B.
--
-- Backfilled to 'a' because supplier A is the only supplier that has ever
-- existed; then the default is dropped, because a row that fills this in for
-- itself is exactly the failure above wearing the constraint's clothes.
--
-- No CHECK on the value, consistent with `issuance_attempts.provider`: the
-- ladder owns that list.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTER TABLE "supplier_requests" ADD COLUMN "provider" text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'a';--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "supplier_requests" ALTER COLUMN "provider" DROP DEFAULT;

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ONE NOTE FOR THE NEXT TASK IN THIS SLICE, recorded here because this file is
-- what makes it true.
--
-- technical-considerations §1.2 quotes the ladder's reserving INSERT as
--
--     values (default, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5, default, default, default, default)
--                                          ^^^^^^^  probe_count
--
-- That `default` was written when `probe_count` was expected to keep its column
-- default. It has none after this migration, so the statement must pass a
-- literal `1` in that position or take a 23502. The `ON CONFLICT (request_id)
-- DO UPDATE SET probe_count = probe_count + 1` half is unaffected — it names
-- the column explicitly and touches nothing else, which is the point of it: on
-- the re-probe path the row may already say `ok`, and resetting `status` to
-- `unknown` would erase the one fact worth having.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
